Myth: Before rivers understood leaving, the world was tucked inside a green drum beneath the roots of a wandering cedar. Its skin was tight, its song unstruck, and every stream curled back to taste its own beginning. In that hush lived Nira, a cricket with emerald knees, who kept a shard of noon hidden in a thimble of bark. It had fallen from the first leaf that dared to shine.

Each evening Nira heard fish sighing in round ponds and children asking where the far hills slept. She climbed the cedar as it walked through darkness and pressed her small foot to the drum.

“Sound,” she begged. “Teach water to go onward.”

The drum muttered, “One shard cannot loosen my green silence.”

“It is small,” said Nira, “but it remembers leaf-vein, sun-road, rain, and thirst.”

She set the noon-shard beneath her wing and chirped until the bark thimble blazed. A golden note split the drumhead. Out rushed the first true current, pulling valleys open, combing stones, and carrying moonlight toward the sea.

Since then, crickets sing beside rivers to keep them brave, and every cedar leans downstream, listening for the drumbeat that began all journeys.
